# udev/99-backup-external.rules
# =============================
#
# Remount the external backup HDD whenever it reappears (dock / re-dock).
#
# The /etc/fstab entry only mounts at BOOT. On a laptop the disk vanishes and
# returns many times between reboots, and each return otherwise leaves it
# attached-but-unmounted -- which makes restic-backup-external.service fail its
# ConditionPathExists and be SKIPPED, which is not a failure: no error, no
# failed unit, no notification, no healthcheck ping.
#
# SOURCE OF TRUTH. Copied root-owned to /etc/udev/rules.d/ by
# scripts/setup-backup.sh (never symlinked -- root's udevd reads it), with the
# placeholders below rendered by scripts/backup-render.sh. Do not hand-edit the
# /etc copy: backup-doctor re-renders this file and compares, so a local edit
# there reports as drift.
#
# Two fields of the rule below are substituted at install time. They are named
# here by FIELD, not by placeholder token, because the renderer rewrites every
# occurrence of a token -- including one inside a comment -- so spelling them out
# would replace this explanation with the values in the only copy an
# administrator ever opens.
#
#   ENV{ID_FS_UUID}     <- BACKUP_EXTERNAL_UUID from ~/.backup.local.
#   ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}  <- systemd-escape -p --suffix=mount of the mount point
#                          (dirname of the repo path). COMPUTED, never hand-
#                          written: a path with a dash needs \x2d and a space
#                          needs \x20, and a wrong unit name yields a rule that
#                          verifies cleanly and then never fires.
#
# The rule only *starts a unit /etc/fstab already defines*, so it can never
# mount anything the fstab entry would not.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_FS_UUID}=="__BACKUP_EXTERNAL_UUID__", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="__BACKUP_EXTERNAL_MOUNT_UNIT__"
